<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Required because the bundle is signed with the hardened runtime.

    Under the hardened runtime an Info.plist usage string is NOT enough: the
    binary must also be entitled to reach the device, or macOS denies the
    request outright. No prompt appears, no TCC row is created, and
    AVCaptureDevice.authorizationStatus reports .denied for a permission the
    user was never asked about. That is exactly what happened here, and it
    began the moment the hardened runtime was switched on alongside the new
    signing identity.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Apple Reminders, for the task list.

    Same trap as the microphone above, and worth repeating because it cost real
    hours there: under the hardened runtime the Info.plist usage string alone
    is not enough. Without this entitlement the request is denied outright —
    no prompt, no TCC row, and an authorization status that reports "denied"
    for a permission the user was never actually asked about.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Contacts, so a phone number in a message can become a person's name.

    Third time this trap is worth naming: under the hardened runtime the
    Info.plist usage string alone is not enough. Without this, CNContactStore
    reports .denied for a permission the user was never shown a prompt for.

    Note what is NOT here and cannot be: Full Disk Access. Reading the Messages
    database needs it, there is no entitlement that grants it, and no API that
    requests it — it is granted by hand in System Settings and nowhere else.
    See MessagesReader.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    The camera, for presence checking during a focus session. OFF BY DEFAULT and
    useless until Nick turns it on — but the entitlement has to be present at
    SIGNING time, not at the moment the feature is switched on, so it ships with
    the bundle either way.

    Fourth time the same trap is worth naming, because it has now cost hours
    twice: under the hardened runtime the Info.plist usage string alone is not
    enough. Without this entitlement AVCaptureDevice reports .denied for a
    permission the user was never shown a prompt for, which looks exactly like
    them having refused it.

    Adding this does NOT turn the camera on and does not cause a prompt. macOS
    asks the first time something actually tries to capture, which is the first
    time Nick enables presence checking. Until then the entitlement sits unused
    and the green light never comes on.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Orion shells out to the openclaw CLI for every agent turn. The hardened
    runtime restricts a signed process from loading or spawning code signed by
    a different authority without these.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
